<section id="delivery"><div class="wrap">
  <h2>How I deliver</h2>
  <p class="lede">Technology delivery from requirement to production, working with architects and engineers rather than around them.</p>
  <div class="projects">
    <article class="card"><h3>Requirements to production</h3><p>I turn business objectives into epics, stories and acceptance criteria, then plan development, integration testing, non-functional requirements, UAT and release as one schedule. Each requirement is traceable through build, test and business acceptance.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>APIs and integrations</h3><p>I keep an integration dependency matrix: provider, consumer, contract status, target date, environment and test owner. API contracts are agreed early so front ends can work against mocks, and nothing is called complete until the real services are tested end to end.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Dependencies and scope</h3><p>Every cross-team dependency has an owner and a required-by date. Scope changes get an impact assessment first. When a late project is asked to add modules, I protect the critical phase and move the rest to a later one.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Partnering with architecture</h3><p>I understand each design well enough to judge its delivery impact. For choices such as Azure or AWS, I help weigh timeline, cost, security, scalability and operating effort, then reflect the decision in the plan.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Release and quality control</h3><p>Releases follow agreed SIT and UAT exit criteria. A critical defect in security or transaction correctness blocks release unless an authorized owner accepts the risk, and I do not bypass pipeline or security gates to hold a date.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Regulated, financial systems</h3><p>Performance, availability, security, resilience, data integrity, observability, auditability and recoverability become measurable acceptance criteria early, and are tested before production. Delivery tooling includes Azure DevOps CI/CD.</p></article>
  </div>
  <h3 style="margin:36px 0 12px">My production release sequence</h3>
  <ol class="chain"><li>Scope</li><li>SIT</li><li>Defect review</li><li>UAT</li><li>Security and environment readiness</li><li>Deployment runbook</li><li>Rollback plan</li><li>Release approval</li><li>Deployment</li><li>Smoke testing</li><li>Business validation</li><li>Monitoring</li><li>Hypercare</li></ol>
</div></section>

